re passing them to `fast-uri`, or route outbound requests against an explicit allowlist of addresses rather than trusting the normalized host.","reported_by":null,"title":"fast-uri vulnerable to server-side request forgery via malformed IPv6 normalization","metadata":null,"cves":["CVE-2026-75975"],"access":"public","severity":"high","module_name":"fast-uri","vulnerable_versions":">=4.0.0 <4.1.3","github_advisory_id":"GHSA-f65p-4m7j-42xc","recommendation":"Upgrade to version 4.1.3 or later","patched_versions":">=4.1.3","updated":"2026-09-02T15:43:32.000Z","cvss":{"score":7.5,"vectorString":"CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:N"},"cwe":["CWE-20","CWE-918"],"url":"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-f65p-4m7j-42xc"}}}
{"type":"auditAdvisory","data":{"resolution":{"id":1158526,"path":"ajv>fast-uri","dev":false,"optional":false,"bundled":false},"advisory":{"findings":[{"version":"4.1.2","paths":["ajv>fast-uri","ajv-formats>ajv>fast-uri"]}],"found_by":null,"deleted":null,"references":"- https://github.com/fastify/fast-uri/security/advisories/GHSA-fph4-wmhf-6fwf\n- https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-75899\n- https://github.com/fastify/fast-uri/commit/2642290672e7211c990aaed3749c2ce667d0af4f\n- https://github.com/fastify/fast-uri/commit/8c15dbe94ec50bb8e595791ebb42c35cef0fd304\n- https://github.com/fastify/fast-uri/commit/ae92a4c5d8c4b6c9e447f048d5fcbde7eebd5514\n- https://cna.openjsf.org/security-advisories.html\n- https://github.com/fastify/fast-uri/releases/tag/v2.4.5\n- https://github.com/fastify/fast-uri/releases/tag/v3.1.6\n- https://github.com/fastify/fast-uri/releases/tag/v4.1.3\n- https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-fph4-wmhf-6fwf","created":"2026-09-02T15:42:39.000Z","id":1158526,"npm_advisory_id":null,"overview":"### Impact\n\n`fast-uri` decodes a hostname's percent escapes twice in a single `normalize()` or `resolve()` call: once during parsing and again during authority recomposition. A nested percent-encoded host therefore survives the first decode and is turned into a live destination by the second, so `normalize('http://%256c%256f%2563%2561%256c%2568%256f%2573%2574/')` returns `http://localhost/`. Applications that normalize or resolve an untrusted URI before an SSRF check, redirect validation, or host allowlist can be steered to a different destination, including internal addresses such as loopback or a cloud metadata endpoint, than the encoded input appeared to contain. This is an incomplete-fix variant of CVE-2026-6322, whose encoded-authority-delimiter fix introduced the second decode.\n\n### Patches\n\nFixed in `fast-uri` 2.4.5, 3.1.6, and 4.1.3.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nReject untrusted URIs whose host component contains an encoded percent sign (`%25`) before passing them to `normalize()` or `resolve()`.","reported_by":null,"title":"fast-uri vulnerable to server-side request forgery via repeated hostname percent-decoding","metadata":null,"cves":["CVE-2026-75899"],"access":"public","severity":"high","module_name":"fast-uri","vulnerable_versions":">=4.0.0 <4.1.3","github_advisory_id":"GHSA-fph4-wmhf-6fwf","recommendation":"Upgrade to version 4.1.3 or later","patched_versions":">=4.1.3","updated":"2026-09-02T15:42:41.000Z","cvss":{"score":7.5,"vectorString":"CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:N"},"cwe":["CWE-174","CWE-918"],"url":"https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-fph4-wmhf-6fwf"}}}
